{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788264626,"nonce":"PFSZmDnw5wgxOIBfOe1gTyAhmsY=","title":"The observation surface hid the cause of death: BloodLoss was cut by the hediff cap, and there is no bleed-out clock","message":"OUT-2 of the M1 post-mortem (`RUNS/m1-20260831/postmortem.md`). Class: **no-signal + mistrusted-signal**, landing at the mod rung — every input is already read, so per DESIGN's 2026-09-01 ruling this cannot stop at prose.\n\n## The measurement\n\nCaptain bled for **11,993 ticks** and died. His health was read four times while he was dying. **`BloodLoss` was CUT from all four reads by the 20-row hediff cap** — `hediffs_more` was 7, 16, 19, 19. The row that named the thing killing him was truncated away, four times, in favour of rows that did not matter.\n\nTable bled for **11,335 ticks** under the same surface.\n\nThere is a second half. Even with `BloodLoss` present, the read gives a severity, not a deadline. The decision the run actually faced at tick 231,968 was \"is there time to walk a rescuer 118 cells\" — roughly a 2,810-tick walk against a ~9,040-tick clock — and **nothing published either number.** The run answered it with a work-priority flip and lost.\n\n## Scope\n\n**Exempt `BloodLoss` from the hediff cap.** A truncation policy that can drop the lethal row is not a byte budget, it is a lie of omission. Investigate whether the right fix is a `BloodLoss` special case or a severity/lethality ordering that puts life-threatening hediffs first and truncates the cosmetic tail — the second is better if it generalises, and it probably does: the same cap can drop a `Hediff_Injury` that is bleeding while keeping scars. Cite what you keyed on.\n\n**Publish `health.ticks_until_bleedout`.** The game computes this — read its own source for the member rather than deriving it from severity (`HediffSet.BleedRateTotal`, and the bleed-out threshold RimWorld itself uses for the \"will bleed out in\" readout). Publishing the game's own number rather than our arithmetic is the difference between a read and a guess.\n\nConsider publishing it alongside a travel estimate for the nearest capable rescuer, since the comparison is the whole decision — but that may belong with the triage procedure (`40ed42f`) instead. Decide and say which.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A pawn with more than 20 hediffs whose `BloodLoss` is present: `BloodLoss` appears in the read. Prove it with a pawn constructed to overflow the cap, not by inspection.\n- `hediffs_more` still reports honestly what was dropped.\n- `health` publishes `ticks_until_bleedout` for a bleeding pawn and omits or nulls it for one who is not.\n- The number matches the game's own readout for the same pawn — compare against the in-game text, do not assert our own formula against itself.\n- Replay Captain's four reads from `RUNS/m1-20260831/` against the new surface and show `BloodLoss` and the clock both present.","files":null}]}